cartridge "SCHNIP/David Florance/1987"

' SCHNIP
' A ColecoVision game based on "Schnip", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk, October
' 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, 2,758 bytes, started by
' schnip.boot with SYS 49152; no author named on the disk). Made for Amy
' Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/schnip.dis; jsc64 shows the court but has no sprite
' collisions, so hits were read from the code), the game is rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision: a squash-style court with a brick front wall, two racket
' players as smooth sprites (BLACK and WHITE, as on the C64), sound
' effects and a title tune.
'
' From the original (orig/schnip.dis):
' - two players (BLACK on controller 1, WHITE on controller 2) run
'   anywhere on the court, eight directions; the ball flies diagonally,
'   bounces off the front wall and both side walls and leaves the court
'   at the back;
' - touching the ball sends it back up toward the front wall, to the
'   side of the player it hit, and gives it the other player's colour:
'   the ball's colour shows who must play it next;
' - when the ball leaves the court, the player whose colour it has loses
'   the point (a ball nobody touched yet, yellow, is replayed);
' - the point's winner serves (presses the button) and must reach his own
'   serve: the new ball has the winner's colour; it starts on the far
'   side and flies up and left; players start each point at the back;
' - first to 21 wins ("BLACK WINS!" / "WHITE WINS!");
' - every 5 points played the level rises and the ball speeds up; the
'   original's speeds cycle: levels 2-4 faster and faster, 5-7 repeat
'   them (kept).
'
' Liberties: the C64 asked each player's name from the keyboard: the
' names are BLACK and WHITE; a hit sends the ball away from the player's
' middle (steeper near the middle, flatter at the edges; the C64 decided
' by a signed byte compare of the left edges, which sent balls hit by the
' racket back across the body); each player touches the ball once per
' 12 frames; a racket that has not moved for 2 s does not return the
' ball (it drops off to the back and the still player loses the point);
' the computer aims its returns flat and away from BLACK; a long rally speeds the ball up a little every 2 hits (so
' every point ends); a serve not taken in 2.5 seconds goes by itself; the
' players move faster than the ball (they matched it on the C64, at
' speeds of 2-5 pixels a frame); the level shows on the court for two
' seconds instead of waiting for F1; one player against a computer WHITE
' that sees the ball 20 frames late (6 on a serve) and carries its motion on and keeps away when the ball is
' BLACK's; the end message, then the title.
'
' Controls: stick moves; a button serves. Title: up/down choose a line,
' left/right change it, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

